WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.630
With a population over 30 million people,
Peru is the 4th biggest country in Latin America

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:10.959
and one of the richest countries in terms
of natural resources and biodiversity in the

00:00:10.959 --> 00:00:11.959
world.

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:17.420
In this vast Andean country we can find, among
many other resources, the biggest silver reserves,

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:23.260
the third biggest zinc and copper reserves,
and the sixth biggest gold reserves in the

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.260
world.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.039
In this video, we’ll talk about Peru.

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:37.260
Ikuchita VisualPolitik!

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.620
Peru is still a poor country, and there is
still a great deal of development to be done

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.190
when it comes to its institutional framework.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:48.930
But despite this, Peru stands out among its
Latin American neighbours as a positive example

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.410
of good reforms.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:55.969
And some even say that, if this process continues,
we will soon be talking about Peru as a great

00:00:55.969 --> 00:00:57.620
Latin American power.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:03.510
Lima, its capital city, has already a bigger
GDP than Bolivia.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.239
Economic Evolution

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:13.759
Peru has recorded in the last decade the second
biggest growth rate in all of Latin America.

00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:18.360
In the same period, its GDP has more than
doubled; the investment rate has gone from

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:23.850
13% of GDP to more than 20%; and the poverty
rate, this is the percentage of people who

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.979
can't afford basic things (food, clothes,
education and health care) has dropped from

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:33.460
rates of over 50% in 2000 to 22% today.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.810
Which is less than half.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:43.290
Not bad at all.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:48.440
And hey!

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:53.720
Peru is one of the least indebted countries
in Latin America and even worldwide!

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.920
Due to a strong determination to use public
funds responsibility, an achievement reached

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:05.610
though the work of the previous governments
of Oyanta Humala, Alan Garcia and Alejandro

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:11.130
Toledo- the public debt has been reduced to
less than 50% since 2000.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:17.430
And today, it represents little more than
20% of GDP.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.430
But, how did Peru's prosperity begin?

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:22.000
Or better still, how did it reach such political
consensus which maintains stability with the

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.370
government's budget while making pro-market
policies?

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:38.330
This is 
probably one of the most interesting things

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:42.780
about Peru, and also where it can teach others
a valuable lesson.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.960
Recently, we’ve made a video talking about
Venezuela…

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:52.190
Interestingly Peru’s economical situation
in the 80’s was even worse than Venezuela’s!

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:56.160
So let’s see what happened.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:02.420
80’s crisis

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:08.820
In the 70s at the beginning of the 80s, the
Peruvian government bet on boosting the economy

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:14.120
through public investment, replacing imports,
and restricting the purchase of foreign products.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.750
To achieve this, they had to borrow money,
which shot up the public debt.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:24.060
The results were so bad that when the oil
crisis happened in 1979 and their economic

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.570
growth slowed down, Peru’s government started
to have so many problems that, in 1983 they

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.270
had suspend payments to their debtors.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:36.480
In other words, they failed to pay their debts
because they ran out of money.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.760
The Peruvian government collapsed!

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:45.040
From that moment things went from bad to worse,
and when Alan Garcia came to power in 1985,

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.430
the country was almost a complete disaster.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:54.400
The government started to intervene on a massive
scale fixing prices, and in the labour market

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.730
they reinforced the import replacement policies
limiting the number of products to available

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:07.370
to buy, and setting an average tariff which
reached, an amazing 66%!

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:15.430
The 
public deficit went through the roof and the

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.140
informal economy became the norm.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:21.750
So, even though there were more than a hundred
taxes, the State couldn’t manage to collect

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:22.750
them.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:26.710
And since the government had collapsed and
nobody wanted to lend them money, they were

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:27.710
in a bad position.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.870
So, what did they do?

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.851
Well, they started to print more and more
money, transforming the Inti [*pronunciation:

00:04:33.851 --> 00:04:40.090
een-tee], - the Peruvian currency, into something
with about the same value as Monopoly money.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:46.211
As if this were not enough, the narco-traffic
and terrorist actions of groups as Sendero

00:04:46.211 --> 00:04:52.540
Luminoso and the MRTA spread out over the
country causing tens of thousands of deaths.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:57.470
The result?

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:10.340
At the end of the decade the GDP had dropped
by 25%; GDP per capita was similar to 1960’s,

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:17.890
annual inflation exceeded 7,500% and the World
Bank recognised Peru as one of the poorest

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:38.230
countries of the world.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:44.180
The situation was so grave that the World
Bank estimates, if it hadn’t happened, Peru’s

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:49.190
GDP and purchasing power parity would today
be similar to Italy’s, Spain’s or South

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:50.190
Korea’s.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.180
No more, no less.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.470
In many ways the Peruvian crisis was very
similar to the one which is facing Venezuela

00:05:56.470 --> 00:06:01.400
today: a lot of public spending, a lot of
taxes, plenty of printing money, combined

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.450
with violence and a drop in commodity prices.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.720
When we released our video about Venezuela’s
crisis many of you asked us what we wiykd

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:09.930
do to solve it.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:14.010
Well, let’s see what Peru did.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:17.270
A WAY TO PROSPERITY

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:24.610
In 1990 a strong plan of reforms and economic
stabilization began.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.280
That same plan still practically persists
today.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.340
It passed through these stages:

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:34.030
In order to slow down inflation, the Inti,
the national currency, was replaced with a

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:42.550
new one, the New Sol and the “dollarization”
(the use of US dollars in their economy) was

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:43.550
allowed in practice.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:48.150
From that moment, monetary stability became
a national priority.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:53.080
The tax system was changed and simplified,
removing 64 taxes.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:57.890
The pension system was restructured, taking,
as a model, the Chilean system of capitalisation,

00:06:57.890 --> 00:06:59.919
but introducing many improvements.

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:06.080
(We’re actually going to talk in depth about
this controversial topic in a future video).

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:12.880
Further, an ambitious program to attract investments
and privatise public utilities was set into

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:13.880
motion.

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:19.639
Soon, companies such as Aeroperu, Electrolima
and the Peruvian Telephone Company were privatised.

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:23.920
A “trade openness strategy” was approved
which has led to a reduction of the average

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:30.370
tariff from the unbelievable 66%, to figures
close to 1,5%, while at the same time numerous

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:36.010
free trade deals were been signed: including
important ones with the US and European Union.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:42.560
In 2002 a gradual decentralization of power
started, which resulted in today most of the

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:46.460
public investment spending belonging to regions
and municipalities.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:54.199
And, finally, probably the most important
point, was their commitment to budgetary stability,

00:07:54.199 --> 00:07:59.360
something that every government since has
respected.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:13.520
Actually, in 1999 a law was approved called
the ‘Fiscal Transparency and Prudence Law’

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:17.480
which restricts the discretionary power of
the governments to spend more than they receive

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:21.720
- including corrective elements for public
deficit situations.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:27.890
Many experts assert that this is the most
important law in the history of Peru.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:32.880
It’s not all roses though, don’t think
that we’ve forgotten Peru’s darkside - the

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:40.390
1990s and Alberto Fujimori’s authoritarianism,
a President that today is in jail serving

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:45.410
a 25 year sentence for crimes against humanity
and corruption.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:51.339
As you can see, and I’m here talking specially
to our friends from Venezuela, it doesn’t

00:08:51.339 --> 00:08:53.190
matter how bad things are.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.870
There’s always a way out.

00:08:55.870 --> 00:09:02.190
PERU TODAY AND TOMORROW

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:07.070
So, why is Peru still a poor country today?

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:11.210
As we said at the beginning of this video,
the institutional shortfalls and challenges

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.860
that this nation are facing are many.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:18.260
Among them, we can’t stress enough the high
degree of corruption and legal uncertainty,

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:23.529
something that we can clearly see with the
“Death Squads case” - Where, under Oyanta

00:09:23.529 --> 00:09:28.310
Humala's government, from 2011 to 2015, a
group of policemen killed innocent people

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:29.310
for money.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:32.800
This group included even a general and a major
from Peru's National Police".

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:37.520
Further, the extremely high levels of crime
and the risk that the mining industry could

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:40.540
entail the same rentier problems that today
Chile suffers.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:45.540
Not to mention the sharp contrast in the living
standards among different regions.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:52.250
On the 5th June, 2016, the second round of
presidential elections was held where Pedro

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:57.290
Pablo Kuczynski was elected as the new President
of the Republic of Peru.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:01.930
These elections were the closest in Peru’s
history and Kuczynski beat out his adversary,

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:06.430
Keiko Fujimori, Alberto Fujimori’s daughter,
by little more than 40,000 votes.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:14.620
The policies announced by Kuczynski include
to keep working on the agenda of pushing competitiveness

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.500
and trade openness.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:22.440
In this regard, he has pledged to reduce tax
to 10% for SMEs (small or medium enterprises),

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:27.720
as well as to battle against bureaucracy,
remove unnecessary regulations; and to pursue

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:36.560
regional economic integrations, similar to
the TPP or the RCEP led by China.

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:41.100
If Kuczynski gets to remain at the helm and
fight all the problems which affect Peru,

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:45.250
despite his relative political weakness in
a not very friendly congress, I’m sure that

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:49.420
the growth of this Andean nation will continue,
and will remain the leader for growth in Latin

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:50.420
America.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:55.000
And maybe, in a few years, Peru (a young nation
with a median age of barely 27) may emulate

00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:02.080
the development of the Asian Dragons and get
transformed into a major global power.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:05.640
So, what do you think?

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:10.090
Will Peru manage to leave poverty behind and
turn into an economic powerhouse?

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:12.380
Is Kuczynski the ideal person to achieve this?

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:20.680
Feel free to leave your opinion in the comments
and subscribe to the channel by clicking here

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:22.200
to receive our new videos.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:23.200
See you!

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.320
Or as we’d say in quechua, TUPANAMCHISKAMA

